U.S. Customs and Border Protection  
National Commodity Specialist Division  
New York, NY

**RE: Request for a binding country of origin ruling — hermetic compressor**

Dear Sir or Madam:

Pursuant to 19 C.F.R. Part 177, [REQUESTER NAME] respectfully requests a binding ruling on the country of origin of hermetic compressor for purposes of marking, determined under the Part 102 rules of origin (19 C.F.R. § 102.11, with the tariff shifts of § 102.20).

CBP previously ruled on the current production setup: origin Mexico (CBP ruling N344002, 6 December 2024). This request concerns a proposed change to that setup: move the motor stator and rotor to China.

## Facts

The stator and rotor are made in China. Final assembly in Mexico.

## Issue

What is the country of origin of hermetic compressor, produced as described above, under the Part 102 rules of origin, 19 C.F.R. § 102.11 with the tariff shifts of § 102.20, for purposes of marking?

This request concerns the origin of the merchandise for marking under the Part 102 rules only. It does not ask CBP to decide the country of origin for Section 301 duties, which is a separate question, and the requester does not contend that marking origin settles it.

## Requester's position

The requester submits that the merchandise is a product of China, for marking under the Part 102 rules. [REQUESTER TO CONFIRM AND EXPAND WITH COUNSEL.]

Tariff-shift check (19 C.F.R. § 102.20): applied to the component codes in the bill of materials, with the good finished in Mexico, the rule gives Mexico (19 C.F.R. § 102.11(a)(3); 19 C.F.R. § 102.20(p), 8414.30). This differs from the earlier ruling cited in support of the change. [CONFIRM WITH COUNSEL]

## Earlier CBP rulings considered

- CBP ruling N340187 — a comparable change where CBP found the origin moved ([rulings.cbp.gov](https://rulings.cbp.gov/ruling/N340187)).

## Attachments

- Bill of materials with country of origin of each component
- Process flow and photographs of each manufacturing step
